 ["Convert RTF to PDF online, free","RTF (Rich Text Format) is the document format of WordPad, Apple TextEdit and many older word processors and legal or medical systems. This free RTF to PDF converter reads .rtf files in your browser and produces searchable PDFs that open on any device – no upload, no sign-up, no watermark."],
 ["How to convert RTF to PDF",{"steps":["Drop one or more .rtf files on the page.","Choose page size, orientation, font size and margins.","Press Convert and download the PDF, or a ZIP for several files."]}],
 ["What is kept from the RTF?",{"table":[["Feature","Kept?"],["Paragraphs and line breaks","Yes"],["Bold text","Yes"],["Accented letters and symbols (\\'xx and \\u escapes)","Yes"],["Fonts, colours, font sizes","No – a clean standard font is used"],["Embedded pictures","No"],["Tables","Text is kept, layout is not"],["Headers and footers","Skipped; page numbers can be added"]]},"The converter is built for text-heavy RTF – letters, notes, reports and exported records. For a layout-perfect copy of a complex RTF with tables and pictures, open it in Word or LibreOffice and use *Save as PDF*, or convert to DOCX first and use [Word to PDF](/word-to-pdf/)."],
 ["What is an RTF file?","Rich Text Format was introduced by Microsoft in 1987 as a way to exchange formatted text between programs. An RTF file is plain text with control words such as \\b for bold and \\par for a new paragraph, which is why almost every editor can open it. It is still used by medical and legal software, macOS TextEdit, and as an export format from e-mail and note apps. PDF is better for sharing because it looks the same everywhere and cannot be edited by accident."],
 ["RTF to PDF on Mac, Windows and phones","On Windows, WordPad (removed from new Windows 11 installs) could print to PDF one file at a time; on Mac, TextEdit can *Export as PDF*. This page converts a whole folder of RTF files at once on any system, including Chromebooks, iPhones and Android phones where no RTF editor is installed."],
 ["Unicode and other languages","Accented Latin, Greek, Cyrillic and other scripts stored with RTF Unicode escapes are decoded and drawn with embedded Noto fonts, so characters do not turn into question marks."],
 ["Archiving old RTF documents","Many offices still hold years of letters, reports and patient or case notes saved as RTF by older software. Converting them to PDF before an old PC is retired makes them readable on any phone, tablet or computer without a word processor, and PDF files are accepted by document-management systems, court portals and e-mail clients alike. Convert a folder at once, keep the original file names, and store the RTF originals alongside the PDFs if you may need to edit them later. Because nothing is uploaded, confidential letters never leave your computer during the conversion."],
 ["Tips",{"list":["If the result looks empty, open the file in a text editor: some \".rtf\" files are really Word .doc files – use [Word to PDF](/word-to-pdf/).","Plain .txt files work too; see [Text to PDF](/text-to-pdf/).","Combine converted letters with [Merge PDF](/merge-pdf/)."]}],
